"Normalizes each category total to the same length so component proportions can be compared independently of total size.\n\nUse when: Relative composition matters more than absolute total differences. Every category has a valid positive total and comparable component definitions.\n\nAvoid when: Sample-size differences are the central fact or denominators are too small for stable shares.\n\nVisual grammar: Every full bar encodes 100% of its category denominator. Segment lengths encode component shares on a common percentage scale.\n\nBuild: Compute each category’s component sum and exclude or explain zero totals. Divide every component by its own category total. Stack shares in a consistent order and annotate original totals or sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check shares sum to 100% within rounding tolerance. Verify the denominator is stated for every category. Distinguish missing components from measured zero shares.\n\nPitfalls: Equal bar lengths conceal different sample sizes unless counts are shown. Interior segments lack a shared baseline, so use separate dot plots for precise share comparisons.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic transport-mode shares across four campuses and display each campus’s respondent count beside its bar.\n\nAlternatives: Stacked bar chart, Mosaic plot, Diverging stacked bar chart."
